WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.067 --> 00:00:01.650
JUSTIN GRAYSTON:
Wait, don't eat that.

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:02.565
MARCO GUIDOBALDI: Why not?

00:00:02.565 --> 00:00:03.840
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: It's rotten.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.350
MARCO GUIDOBALDI: Oh,
I guess you're right.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:09.540
I bought it today, but I didn't
really check every single apple

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.750
in the bag.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.450
It's such a lot of work to
make sure all apples are good.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:17.130
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: It can be,
unless you can get some help

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:18.215
from machine learning.

00:00:18.215 --> 00:00:19.090
MARCO GUIDOBALDI: Ah.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:20.590
JUSTIN GRAYSTON:
With AutoML Vision,

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:23.430
you can train a visual
recognition model quickly

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.810
and easily, using
your own pictures,

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.740
so you can better automate
this kind of thing.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:29.882
Look.

00:00:29.882 --> 00:00:30.724
[BUZZER]

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:32.470
See?

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:34.300
It's 100% rotten.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.710
MARCO GUIDOBALDI:
Wow, this makes

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.170
quality control look so easy.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.420
Can you show me how it's done?

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:40.587
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Sure thing.

00:00:40.587 --> 00:00:41.801
[WHIRRING OF THE LOGO]

00:00:41.801 --> 00:00:44.603
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.960
I built this web
app using a model

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.970
trained on pictures of fruit.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:54.780
Since we've trained it on a
data set of good and bad apples,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.550
when I take a picture
of a new apple,

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.850
it's able to accurately figure
out if it's good or bad,

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.560
like I did for your apple.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.530
The model clearly
recognized it as bad.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.330
MARCO GUIDOBALDI:
That is really cool.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.782
How would I build one myself?

00:01:08.782 --> 00:01:10.740
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Let me
show you how it's done.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.830
It all starts in the
Google Cloud console.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:17.970
First things first, we need
data to train the model on.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.490
Here, we've got a Google Cloud
Storage bucket full of images.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.680
The images are divided into
rotten and fresh folders.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.930
I've created a
CSV that lists all

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.990
of the images and their labels.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.660
I've used a quick
script to do that,

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:34.490
as there are
thousands of images,

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:38.730
but you can use the AutoML
interface to do this manually.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:42.060
Let's take a look at
the interface now.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.850
Here, we need to
create a data set.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.530
It's really easy.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.470
Go to New Data Set,
give it a name,

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.980
choose how you want to
import your images--

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.860
in our case, I'm just going
to point it at the CSV--

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.672
and then hit Create.

00:01:57.672 --> 00:02:00.150
It will import them from GCS.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.060
This may take a few minutes.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.240
Now that we have all the
images imported and labeled,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.660
we can train the model on them.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.400
AutoML takes care of
splitting the data set

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.430
for training, validation,
and testing, so you

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.380
don't have to worry about that.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:21.630
Just hit Start Training and let
Google Cloud handle the rest.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.210
This can take anywhere from
15 minutes to several hours,

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.600
depending on the
size of the data set,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.690
so let's switch to a model
that's already trained.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:33.460
Once you have
trained your model,

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:37.930
you can test the prediction
right here in the interface.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:42.790
Just upload a picture
and look at the result.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.160
There is an API
endpoint, too, and that's

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.810
what I link my app to.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:51.100
Simple-- no service to
manage, no code to train.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.030
MARCO GUIDOBALDI:
That is fantastic.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.640
Now, I can easily train my own
visual models for apples, food,

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.560
or just about anything else.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.330
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Exactly.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.160
If you like this
demo, don't forget

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:06.340
to subscribe for more great
Google Cloud Platform content.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.840
[WHIRRING OF THE LOGO]

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.890
[THEME MUSIC]

